# GATE-K8REACH registry — every UNAOS_* knob with no K8_FEATS arm, and why.
# NA <reason> = deliberately absent from the Pi bare-metal image.
# TODO <owner/note> = grandfathered at seeding, nobody has ruled yet.
# `scripts/k8-reach.py --evidence <KNOB>` prints the sites behind a row.
#
# AN `NA` ROW CITES THE COMMAND THAT JUSTIFIED IT, not a reason string alone (pi 7,
# 2026-09-06). A human first pass inherits the classifier's blind spots exactly: the
# arch-neutral file (`nvidia-kepler`'s site in `video/wm.rs`) and the x86-gated callee
# (`rastmc`) are the cases a reader also mis-sorts, because the path and the cfg both
# look decisive and neither is. `NA  x86 only` is a second classifier with no proof and
# worse recall; `NA  --evidence: 11 sites, 0 Pi-live (all under drivers/gpu, x86 mod tree)`
# is checkable by the next seat.
UNAOS_AHCI   NA   --evidence: ahci 17 sites, 0 Pi-live, 0 negated — 16 classified X86 and 1 PROSE (the module doc quoting its own gate). Every code site is the SAME conjunction, `all(target_arch = "x86_64", feature = "ahci")`: the `pub mod ahci` declaration in drivers/mod.rs, the AHCI registry block at the tail of drivers/block.rs, and the one enumeration hook appended in arch/x86_64/pci.rs. There is no arch-neutral consumer to mis-sort (the `nvidia-kepler`-in-`video/wm.rs` trap this file's header names) and no x86-gated callee reached from a Pi-live caller (the `rastmc` trap): drivers/ahci.rs is not lexed on aarch64 at all, so a K8_FEATS arm would arm nothing. Not merely absent from the Pi image but UNBUILDABLE there as written — the enumeration seam is `arch::pci` config space, which the aarch64 tree does not provide. The FILE is deliberately arch-neutral in name and shape (AHCI exists on aarch64 boards), so if an aarch64 board ever grows an ABAR route this row is the place that has to change. Added 2026-09-15 with the driver's first rung (rmbp-ledger B89).
UNAOS_AHCI_WRITE   NA   --evidence: ahci-write 26 sites, 0 Pi-live, 0 negated, implies ahci — every one is inside the same `target_arch = "x86_64"` conjunction the `UNAOS_AHCI` row above documents, and `ahci-write` implies `ahci` in Cargo.toml, so a K8_FEATS arm for it would arm its parent too and still reach no aarch64 code. The three code homes: the `write_block_at` tail of drivers/ahci.rs (a file the aarch64 tree never lexes — its `pub mod` is `#[cfg]`-erased), the `WriteGrant` + `write_sectors_granted` tail of drivers/block.rs (`all(target_arch = "x86_64", feature = "ahci-write")` on every item), and the SATA leg of install/partition.rs, which is `installdemo`-gated at its `pub mod` declaration and therefore not in a Pi image at all — K8_FEATS is `baremetal,skip_xhci` plus witness/logts/bootlog and has never carried `installdemo`. The two arms in install/mod.rs are single `#[cfg(all(target_arch = "x86_64", ...))]` match arms in a file aarch64 does compile, so they are the only sites in an aarch64-lexed file and both vanish. Not merely absent from the Pi image but UNBUILDABLE there as written, for the reason the parent row gives: the enumeration seam is `arch::pci` config space. If an aarch64 board ever grows an ABAR route, this row and its parent change together. Added 2026-09-15 with the driver's SATA write half (rmbp-ledger B89).
UNAOS_BAR1EXP              TODO  seeded 2026-09-06; no Pi-live cfg site
UNAOS_BAR1WEDGE   NA   --evidence: bar1wedge 21 sites 0 Pi-live 0 NEG [implies: nvidia-kepler]. Every site but one is in drivers/gpu/pcihealth.rs; the exception is the SECSTA2 call statement appended to arch/x86_64/pci.rs, which carries the full all(target_arch = "x86_64", feature = "bar1wedge") conjunction in its own attribute (the AHCI hook shape), so a Pi compile still emits nothing and every one is x86-qualified: the two call statements sit inside `#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]` functions (census, rp_at_wedge) and all sixteen tail items carry `all(target_arch = "x86_64", feature = "bar1wedge")` in the attribute itself, so a Pi compile emits nothing for this knob and there is nothing for a K8_FEATS arm to arm. The subject is a PCIe root port's config space reached through ACPI MCFG ECAM — the BCM2711 publishes no MCFG and this kernel's aarch64 half has no CF8/ECAM accessor at all, so the rung is not merely unbuilt on the Pi, it has no hardware to address there. Registered 2026-09-15 by the BAR1WEDGE executor (rmbp, ledger A1).
UNAOS_BCMARECON            TODO  seeded 2026-09-06; no Pi-live cfg site
UNAOS_BCMAS1               TODO  seeded 2026-09-06; no Pi-live cfg site
UNAOS_BEAM   NA   --evidence: beam 12 sites 11 Pi-live 1 NEG [implies: jd1dc]; Cargo.toml `beam = ["jd1dc"]` (the guard and the aperture are that rung's; UNAOS_JD1DC is the TODO row above). Ten of the eleven Pi-live sites are in arch/aarch64/display_tegra.rs, a module declared `#[cfg(feature = "tegra")] pub mod display_tegra` (arch/aarch64/mod.rs:111) — a Pi image never lexes the file; the eleventh is arch/aarch64/mod.rs:562, conjoined `all(feature = "tegra", feature = "beam")`, whose `not(...)` twin is the NEG. The consumer, video/beam.rs, is unconditional and arch-neutral on purpose (it is the MECHANISM, not a knob site) and folds to a no-op wherever `arch::scanout_beam()` is the constant `None` — which on the Pi it is: the BCM2711 HVS publishes no raster-line register this kernel reads, so there is nothing for the knob to arm there. The register it locks is Tegra234 nvdisplay `NV_PDISP_RG_DPCA` under the display@ BPMP power guard.
