# ---- rows for knobs that are NOT in THIS branch's `_feats` map -------------------------
# Measured and written by orin 17 (2026-09-06) against `hw-jetson` 963516c8, handed over ccd
# with the `--evidence` output behind each. They are DEFERRED here and become reds on the
# trunk if the knob has still not arrived; see the STALE note in k8-reach.py.
UNAOS_GA10B_PROBE2   NA   --evidence: ga10bprobe2 19 sites 19 Pi-live; Cargo.toml:2025 `ga10bprobe2 = ["tegra"]` — the knob arms `tegra` transitively, so a Pi image would build the wrong SoC; the payload module is gated `all(feature = "tegra", any(ga10bprobe1, ga10bprobe2, ga10bprobe3))` (arch/aarch64/mod.rs:110) and its call site is inside the tegra boot path (main.rs:2254), so all 19 sites are empty without `tegra`. GA10B is Tegra234 iGPU silicon; the Pi 4's GPU is VideoCore VI (`v3d`, already armed in K8_FEATS).
UNAOS_GA10B_PROBE3   NA   --evidence: ga10bprobe3 56 sites 56 Pi-live, ga10bprobe3b 9 sites 8 Pi-live; Cargo.toml:2048 `ga10bprobe3 = ["tegra"]` and :2065 `ga10bprobe3b = ["ga10bprobe3"]` — same tegra closure as PROBE2, same module gate (arch/aarch64/mod.rs:110) and same tegra-path call site (main.rs:2254). Rungs 3/3b live in arch/aarch64/ga10b_probe.rs (62 of the 65 sites); no Pi hardware answers a GA10B BAR.
UNAOS_GA10B_PROBE4   NA   --evidence: ga10bprobe4a 51 sites 50 Pi-live, ga10bprobe4b 10 sites 9 Pi-live, ga10bprobe4c 43 sites 43 Pi-live, ga10bprobe4d 10 sites 10 Pi-live (orin 27, 2026-09-12: values 3 and 4 added); same tegra closure as PROBE3: Cargo.toml `ga10bprobe4a = ["tegra"]`, `ga10bprobe4b = ["ga10bprobe4a"]`, `ga10bprobe4c = ["ga10bprobe4b"]`, `ga10bprobe4d = ["ga10bprobe4c"]`, same module gate (arch/aarch64/mod.rs) and same tegra-path call site (main.rs); rungs 4a/4b/4c and the deferred arm live in arch/aarch64/ga10b_probe.rs, their DMA window seater in arch/aarch64/mmu_tegra.rs, and 4d's one hook in power.rs (`psci_call`, cfg ga10bprobe4d, aarch64 non-pi); no Pi hardware answers a GA10B BAR.
UNAOS_GA10B_PROBE5   NA   --evidence: ga10bprobe5 3 sites 3 Pi-live (the arch-neutral LOADER `arch/aarch64/ga10b_fw.rs`, Cargo.toml `ga10bprobe5 = []`); its sub-knobs ga10bprobe5a/5e/5b/5d are added by a `case` map the classifier does not parse, and each `= ["ga10bprobe5a"...]` closes over `ga10bprobe4a = ["tegra"]` — the same tegra closure as PROBE2/3/4 (orin-0912b, 2026-09-12). NOT in kernel8()`\s curated K8_FEATS: the loader module compiles arch-neutrally but its only run sites are `fixture_service` (witness+ga10bprobe5, and every Pi media image is witness-FREE) and `ga10b_ignite::ga10bprobe5_run` (tegra); no Pi hardware answers a GA10B BAR, so setting the knob and flashing kernel8 changes nothing.
UNAOS_NET5   NA   --evidence: net5 30 sites 29 Pi-live, net4 9 sites 8 Pi-live, pcie3 28 sites 26 Pi-live, pcie2 17 sites 16 Pi-live; the Cargo closure net5->net4->pcie3->pcie2 pulls NO `tegra` (Cargo.toml:1000/980/965/951), so unlike the other six this one WOULD compile into a Pi image — and reach nothing: the armed entry `net4_bringup` is `all(feature = "net4", feature = "tegra")` (main.rs:2357), and the only non-tegra entry (main.rs:351) sits inside the QEMU-virt block `all(target_arch = "aarch64", not(feature = "pi"), not(feature = "tegra"))` (main.rs:309) which a kernel8 image compiles out because `baremetal = ["pi", "aarch64_el0"]` (Cargo.toml:225). Arming it would add arch/aarch64/rtl8168_tegra.rs + pcie_probe.rs + mmu_tegra.rs to the image with zero call sites. Hardware: the RTL8168 hangs off Tegra234 PCIe C5; the Pi 4's PCIe carries the VL805 xHCI and its NIC is the on-SoC GENET (UNAOS_GENET is already armed in K8_FEATS).
UNAOS_SDMMCWRITE   NA   --evidence: sdmmcwrite 17 sites 17 Pi-live, sdmmc 50 sites 48 Pi-live; same closure as SDMMCROOT — every site is in the `tegra`-gated `mod metal` (sdmmc_tegra.rs:127), the export is `all(feature = "tegra", feature = "sdmmcwrite")` (sdmmc_tegra.rs:125) and the call site is `all(feature = "sdmmcwrite", feature = "tegra")` (main.rs:2371), so a Pi image reaches none of the 17. NOT registered on a "the Pi cannot write" ground: since U9 drivers/block.rs:1198 routes SD writes to `crate::drivers::emmc2::write_block_512` (implemented at drivers/emmc2.rs:798), so the Pi card path DOES write — the refusal claim in the emmc2.rs header (lines 6-7) is stale prose. The gap-#3 probe targets a Tegra234 microSD slot the Pi does not have.
UNAOS_TCUPROBE   NA   --evidence: tcuprobe 2 sites 2 Pi-live, tegra 270 sites 259 Pi-live; the knob arms `tegra` outright in both arroyo (`_feats="${_feats}tcuprobe,tegra,"`, arroyo:1328) and Cargo (`tcuprobe = ["tegra"]`, Cargo.toml:2658) — a Pi image carrying it would build the wrong SoC (boot_tegra/mmu_tegra/smmu_tegra, tegra GIC and serial). Both sites are tegra-conjoined: the module `all(feature = "tegra", feature = "tcuprobe") pub mod hsp_tegra` (arch/aarch64/mod.rs:110) and the arm `hsp_tegra::tcuprobe_arm` (main.rs:2495). The Tegra234 HSP shared mailbox / TCU has no BCM2711 counterpart.
UNAOS_TCURX   NA   --evidence: tcurx 32 sites 32 Pi-live, tcuprobe 2 sites 2 Pi-live, orinrx 10 sites 10 Pi-live, tegra 270 sites 259 Pi-live; arms `tegra` in arroyo (`_feats="${_feats}tcurx,tcuprobe,orinrx,tegra,"`, arroyo:1342) and in Cargo (`tcurx = ["tegra", "orinrx", "tcuprobe"]`, Cargo.toml:2697) — same wrong-SoC bar as TCUPROBE. Its consumer half is the Tegra234 UART/HSP RX drain (arch/aarch64/serial.rs, arch/aarch64/hsp_tegra.rs, xusb_tegra.rs:1936); the Pi's console is the BCM2711 PL011/mini-UART, a different driver entirely.
UNAOS_VIRT_EL0             NA    --evidence at orin 17's ccf93304: virt_el0 29 sites, 14 Pi-live, 11 NEG [implies: aarch64_el0]; a QEMU-virt-only board term implying nothing a Pi image wants, and the majority of its sites are `not(any(baremetal, tegra_el0, virt_el0))` exclusions — an exclusion term is not reach (the NEG verdict exists because this knob's first evidence run said 25 Pi-live)
UNAOS_VIRT_TICK   NA   --evidence (rmbp 18, 2026-09-10, at the hw-jetson fold): virt_tick 28 sites, 24 Pi-live, 4 NEG [implies: aarch64_el0, virt_el0]. It IMPLIES `virt_el0`, which is already NA one row above as a QEMU-virt-only board term — a knob cannot reach a Pi image with a term the image never carries. Same exclusion-term caveat as VIRT_EL0: most Pi-live hits are `any(feature = "tegra", feature = "virt_tick")` alternations in `arch/aarch64/exceptions.rs` where the Pi takes the `not(...)` arm.
UNAOS_NOBSPRUN    NA   --evidence: bsprun 10 sites, 9 Pi-live, 1 NEG — but reach is decided at the PRODUCER, not the consumer, and `grep -n 'UNAOS_NOBSP' unaos/arroyo` finds FOUR live reads, all Jetson-only: arroyo:880-881 are `[ -n "${UNAOS_TEGRA:-}" ]`-guarded, and arroyo:5252/5257 are inside `esp_jetson()` (the Jetson media builder). kernel8() enters NEITHER path and never puts `bsprun` in the feature list at all, so the knob cannot change a byte of the Pi image. Ruled NA by orin 25 (owner) 2026-09-10; the two esp_jetson sites are rmbp 18's addition to their reason, which said UNAOS_TEGRA-guarded ONLY — that is incomplete, and esp_jetson's own comment says why: it forces `tegra` WITHOUT setting the env var the mapping line keys on. `sched.rs:10465` is not a counter-example: it is a `compile_error!` rejecting bsprun-without-bsptick on every arch, which is why it carries no tegra term.
UNAOS_NOBSPTICK   NA   --evidence: same producer family as UNAOS_NOBSPRUN (the tool resolves both to `bsprun`: 10 sites, 9 Pi-live, 1 NEG). Same four Jetson-only reads, same command: `grep -n 'UNAOS_NOBSP' unaos/arroyo`. Ruled NA by orin 25 (owner) 2026-09-10, reason completed by rmbp 18 with the two `esp_jetson()` sites.
UNAOS_APSRUN      NA   --evidence: ONE live read, arroyo:1402, writing `_feats` (`apsrun,tegrasmp,tegra`). `kernel8()` builds its OWN curated feature list — `local K8_FEATS="baremetal,skip_xhci"` at arroyo:6249, extended only by its own explicit arms — and NEVER reads the `_feats` knob-mapping block. So a knob that writes `_feats` cannot change a byte of a kernel8 image regardless of its guards. Command: `grep -n 'K8_FEATS=' unaos/arroyo` (every assignment is inside kernel8(), none from _feats). Ruled NA by orin 25 (owner) 2026-09-10.
UNAOS_NOAPSRUN    NA   --evidence: TWO live reads: arroyo:1403 is UNAOS_TEGRA-guarded and writes `_feats`; arroyo:5627 is inside `esp_jetson()` (opens 5608), the Jetson media builder — the same unguarded-inside-esp_jetson shape rmbp 18 caught me missing on UNAOS_NOBSPRUN. kernel8() enters neither. `kernel8()` builds its OWN curated feature list — `local K8_FEATS="baremetal,skip_xhci"` at arroyo:6249, extended only by its own explicit arms — and NEVER reads the `_feats` knob-mapping block. So a knob that writes `_feats` cannot change a byte of a kernel8 image regardless of its guards. Command: `grep -n 'K8_FEATS=' unaos/arroyo` (every assignment is inside kernel8(), none from _feats). Ruled NA by orin 25 (owner) 2026-09-10.
UNAOS_NOUSBLUN    NA   --evidence, re-run at this commit and quoted verbatim: `nousblun: 12 site(s), 1 Pi-live, 11 negated`, the one PI-LIVE being `drivers/xhci/mod.rs:12169  #[cfg(feature = "nousblun")]` — the census-SKIPPED witness inside `bring_up_storage` (the line number is as of this commit and drifts; re-run `scripts/k8-reach.py --evidence UNAOS_NOUSBLUN` rather than trusting it). ONE live read in the producer, arroyo:1453, writing `_feats` (`nousblun`). `kernel8()` builds its OWN curated feature list — `local K8_FEATS="baremetal,skip_xhci"` at arroyo:6608, extended only by its own explicit arms — and NEVER reads the `_feats` knob-mapping block, so a knob that writes `_feats` cannot change a byte of a kernel8 image. Command: `grep -n 'K8_FEATS=' unaos/arroyo` (every assignment is inside kernel8(), none from _feats); `grep -n 'UNAOS_NOUSBLUN' unaos/arroyo` (one non-comment line, :1453). SECOND REASON, independent of the first and the one that would still hold if the mapping block were ever wired into kernel8: all 12 cfg sites are in `drivers/xhci/mod.rs`, and `K8_FEATS` carries `skip_xhci` unconditionally — a Pi image built by `kernel8()` does not bring up xHCI at all unless the operator arms `UNAOS_PIUSB`, which extends K8_FEATS by its own arm and still never adds `nousblun`, so the PI-LIVE site is unreachable in an image whose feature list cannot contain the feature. Ruled NA by orin 27 (executor, USBLUN-M3) 2026-09-12; counts and PI-LIVE site corrected in M4 after the second addendum review (G3) found the row saying nine sites where the tool said twelve.
UNAOS_FACET       NA   --evidence: ONE live read, arroyo:1225, writing `_feats` (`facet`). `kernel8()` builds its OWN curated feature list — `local K8_FEATS="baremetal,skip_xhci"` at arroyo:6249, extended only by its own explicit arms — and NEVER reads the `_feats` knob-mapping block. So a knob that writes `_feats` cannot change a byte of a kernel8 image regardless of its guards. Command: `grep -n 'K8_FEATS=' unaos/arroyo` (every assignment is inside kernel8(), none from _feats). Ruled NA by orin 25 (owner) 2026-09-10.
UNAOS_NET6        NA   --evidence, re-run at this commit and quoted verbatim: `net6: 51 site(s), 43 Pi-live, 7 negated` — and the 43 are REAL, not a classifier artifact: `net6 = ["dep:smoltcp"]` (Cargo.toml) pulls NO board term, and its sites are in `syscall.rs` / `shell.rs` / `net_phy.rs`, every one of which a `kernel8` image does compile. So unlike UNAOS_NET5 (which at least stops at a `tegra`-gated entry point) this knob WOULD compile into a Pi image — the whole socket family, the three shell-verb bodies and the persistent stack — and REACH NOTHING, because the surface is driven entirely by a registered device adapter and the Pi has none. `net6::register_nic` has exactly TWO callers, `arch/aarch64/virtio_net.rs` (`#[cfg(feature = "vnet")]`, QEMU virt) and `arch/aarch64/rtl8168_tegra.rs` (`#[cfg(feature = "net4")]`, Tegra234 PCIe C5), and neither feature is in K8_FEATS; the Pi's NIC is the on-SoC BCM2711 GENET (`UNAOS_GENET`, already armed there) and `genet.rs` registers no `NicOps`. With no adapter, `net6::init()` prints `stack NOT up — no NIC adapter registered` and every verb and every one of the seven syscalls fails closed. Arming it on a Pi would therefore add code and change no behaviour, which is the exact shape SR1 forbids advertising as reachable. WHAT WOULD CHANGE THIS ROW: a `genet` device adapter (four function pointers, the `virtio_net.rs` shape) plus `net6` in K8_FEATS — a pi-lane fold, not a jetson one, and the reason the surface was written adapter-shaped in the first place.
UNAOS_FTDIRX      NA   --evidence, re-run at this commit and quoted verbatim: `ftdirx: 7 site(s), 7 Pi-live, 0 negated` — one in `drivers/xhci/ftdi.rs` (the tail `ftdirx` module) and six in `drivers/xhci/mod.rs` (the completion claim at :4368, three teardown drops at :6278/:6486/:14658, the service call at :13362, and the tail `impl XhciController` at :15866; line numbers drift — re-run `scripts/k8-reach.py --evidence UNAOS_FTDIRX` rather than trusting them). ONE live read in the producer, arroyo, writing `_feats` (`ftdirx`). TWO INDEPENDENT REASONS, the same pair UNAOS_NOUSBLUN's row establishes for this exact file. FIRST: `kernel8()` builds its OWN curated feature list — `local K8_FEATS="baremetal,skip_xhci"`, extended only by its own explicit arms — and NEVER reads the `_feats` knob-mapping block, so a knob that writes `_feats` cannot change a byte of a kernel8 image. Command: `grep -n 'K8_FEATS=' unaos/arroyo` (every assignment is inside kernel8(), none from _feats); `grep -n 'UNAOS_FTDIRX' unaos/arroyo` (one non-comment line). SECOND, and it would still hold if the mapping block were ever wired into kernel8: every site is in the xHCI driver, `K8_FEATS` carries `skip_xhci` unconditionally, and even `UNAOS_PIUSB` (which extends K8_FEATS by its own arm) never adds `ftdirx` — so the PI-LIVE sites are unreachable in an image whose feature list cannot contain the feature. THIRD, the physical one: the code drives an FTDI FT232 USB-serial cable, which is the 2012 rMBP's ONLY console because that board has no 16550; a Pi has a PL011 on its header and no FTDI to enumerate, so there is nothing for the knob to arm there even with xHCI up. Ruled NA by the rmbp FTDIRX executor (rmbp-ledger A9) 2026-09-15.
UNAOS_KEPLER_USERD_SNOOP  NA   --evidence, re-run at this commit and quoted verbatim: `nvidia-kepler-userdsnoop: 2 site(s), 0 Pi-live, 0 negated  [implies: nvidia-kepler]` — both in `drivers/gpu/kepler.rs` (the pre-init arm and its witness-conditional restore); line numbers drift, so re-run `scripts/k8-reach.py --evidence UNAOS_KEPLER_USERD_SNOOP` rather than trusting them. ONE live read in the producer, arroyo, writing `_feats` (`nvidia-kepler-userdsnoop`). THREE INDEPENDENT REASONS, the first two being the pair UNAOS_NOUSBLUN's row establishes for this exact file. FIRST: `kernel8()` builds its OWN curated feature list — `local K8_FEATS="baremetal,skip_xhci"` at arroyo:7111, extended only by its own explicit arms — and NEVER reads the `_feats` knob-mapping block, so a knob that writes `_feats` cannot change a byte of a kernel8 image. Command: `grep -n 'K8_FEATS=' unaos/arroyo` (every assignment is inside kernel8(), none from _feats). SECOND, and it would still hold if the mapping block were ever wired into kernel8: the feature implies `nvidia-kepler` (Cargo.toml), `nvidia-kepler` is in no K8_FEATS arm, and `drivers/gpu/mod.rs` declares `kepler`/`kepler_display` under `#[cfg(feature = "nvidia-kepler")]` — a Pi image never lexes either file, which is why the tool reports 0 Pi-live. THIRD, the physical one: this is a probe rung on the 2012 rMBP's GK107 (GeForce GT 650M) — an MMIO experiment against NVIDIA PFIFO/PDISPLAY registers on a discrete PCIe GPU. A Pi 4 has a BCM2711 with a VideoCore VI and no NVIDIA part on any bus, so there is nothing for the knob to arm there even if both reasons above were lifted. Ruled NA by the rmbp SHUTRESTORE executor (rmbp-queue GPU LADDERS, R19 restoration) 2026-09-15.
